      "source_number": "1062",
      "original_required_task": "Prove the square-computation rule for a two-digit number with tens digit n and units digit5: multiply n by n+1 and append25.",
      "actual_explicit_subparts": 1,
      "source_mathematics_status": "solved",
      "domain": "Ordinary base-ten two-digit positive integer 10n+5, with n an integer in{1,...,9}. The units digit is exactly5.",
      "independent_solution": "Write the number as10n+5. Then(10n+5)^2=100n^2+100n+25=100n(n+1)+25. Appending the two digits25 to the ordinary decimal representation of n(n+1) means multiplying that preceding integer by100 and adding25. Since0<=25<100, those final two digits are exactly25 with no carry into the preceding block. Thus the stated algorithm produces the square for every permitted tens digit n=1,...,9.",
      "exact_derivation_steps": [
        "(10n+5)^2=(10n)^2+2(10n)(5)+5^2.",
        "This equals100n^2+100n+25=100n(n+1)+25.",
        "Appending a two-digit suffix25 has numeric value100 times the preceding integer plus25; hence the suffix description and formula coincide."
      ],
      "boundary_checks_optional_not_new_tasks": [
        "At n=1:15^2=225, obtained by appending25 to1*2=2.",
        "At n=9:95^2=9025, obtained by appending25 to9*10=90.",
        "n=0 would give the one-digit number5, outside the original two-digit task, even though the polynomial identity itself still holds."
      ],
      "qualifications": "No extra equality-identification, uniqueness or extension-to-other-digit task is requested. The append rule is a base-ten positional identity, not an unexplained string operation.",
      "unresolved_material_ambiguity": null
    },
